From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yimin Yuan <yimin.yuan.93@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:31:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Windows: Track self-modifying code at host page
 granularity

Under Wine on hosts with pages larger than 4KiB (e.g. 16KiB on Apple Silicon), page protections only apply to whole host pages, so trapping individual 4KiB pages of RWX intervals left them writable whenever another page of their host page was, and untrapping a faulting page made its whole host page writable while only that page was invalidated; the resulting undetected writes left stale code running, crashing JITs such as V8. Query the host page size through the unix library, trap, untrap and invalidate whole host pages, and single step inline self-modifying code anywhere in the untrapped host page. A host page whose other pages hold data would fault on every write to that data, so once a write faults on a page no code was compiled from, or the host page contains writable pages outside of RWX intervals, stop trapping it and validate code from it before execution instead: ExecutableRangeInfo reports such ranges, the frontend enables full SMC detection for blocks with code from them, and the disk cache neither stores nor reuses such code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
